The beauty of it is... (as all you guys who have
worked in teams of dozens of programmers will know)
that 95% of us don't get a choice anyway.

If a company is worth its salt and is serious about
large, maintainable code bases, then  they are going to
have a pretty strict coding standard laid down by
someone else.  And often they aren't really looking for
a lot of debate over it.  You will follow the coding
standard, you will have code reviews to ensure you are
following the coding standard.

If you don't like it, don't let the door hit you on
your ass on the way out. (IFYDLI...)

Whether the coding method is the best or sub-par,
there is definitely an advantage for everyone buying into
the *same* standard so you don't have to know the peculiarities
of each individual coder to be able to maintain their code.

This leads to some formatting such as Kent put forward, that,
to a lot of us looks ugly.  But you know what? IFYDLI...
The objective is that every source file written should
look the same, style-wise, to all other source files in the
same project, regardless of the author.
If you are stepping into a large existing code base, the
alternative of changing the entire code base to conform to
your idea of the "best coding standard" is just stupid.

So... most coders are not going to have the luxury of
setting the standards by which they work.  IFYDLI...

So, while these conversations are fun (you guys are having
fun, right?) and may be useful when you are all the "software
manager" they are not related to nethack, nor are they
relevant to a lot of practical coding situations.

1. if you are writing code in a large code base - conform
to the standard
2. if you are writing code for a small code base that will
not be maintained by others - who cares what you use
3. if you are starting a code base that you think will
develop into a large code base, ok, then be careful, think
long and hard, and ask people with experience (like Kent :)
I completely agree with him that K&R just doesn't cut it as
a style guide).

BTW, one guy I work with here who bucks the trend sez:

"It was hard to write, it should be hard to understand."

Needless to say we are slowly removing all of his code out
of the software... the only problem is he by far the best
optimized coder I've ever met.

You can all pity me.  I now have to use Hungarian notation.  I
don't like it, but I have to - its the standard they use here.
And, you know, since everyone else is using it (and
consequently using the notation as a crutch) then I better damn
well use it too.

Don the blathering conformist

